Amputation
Disabilities & Congenital Disorders
Absence of limb, Phantom limb pain, Residual stump neuroma

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DCD-001
Reason for Visit: Acute exacerbation of residual stump pain with new-onset skin breakdown and suspected localized infection.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated sudden worsening of stump erythema, exquisite localized tenderness, and uncontrolled burning phantom limb pain over the past 4 hours. Assessment could not be safely delayed until the next in-hours period due to the high risk of rapidly progressive cellulitis or osteomyelitis in a compromised limb requiring immediate intervention and pain management.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My stump is burning and rubbing raw, and the old leg feels like it is cramping violently.
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a 24-hour history of increasing friction and contact dermatitis over the residual limb stump due to prosthetic use, which rapidly evolved this evening into severe, localized exquisite tenderness. This was followed by an acute, severe flare of phantom limb pain, described as a burning and cramping sensation in the missing extremity. Skin breakdown and a small ulceration were noted by the spouse 4 hours ago. Symptoms are compounded by asymmetrical gait biomechanics today, leading to compensatory lower back pain.
Functional Status: Ambulation currently limited; using wheelchair due to inability to bear weight on the prosthesis.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of falls due to altered mobility and severe pain distraction.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/88 mmHg | HR: 98 bpm | Temp: 37.6 C | RR: 18 bpm | SpO2: 97% | Wt: 82.0 Kg
General: Patient is visibly distressed, grimacing, and guarding the residual limb.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular and respiratory systems unremarkable. Musculoskeletal exam reveals an absence of the left lower limb below the knee. The residual stump exhibits a 2cm superficial ulceration with surrounding 4cm erythema and warmth. Exquisite localized tenderness is palpated at the lateral aspect of the stump, consistent with residual stump neuroma irritation. Muscle atrophy is noted in the adjacent proximal joint. No crepitus or fluctuance. Contralateral limb exam is unremarkable.
Environmental Observations: Dimly lit hallway with scattered rugs posing trip hazards; heating is adequate.
Medication Reconciliation: Analgesic usage checked against EHR; patient has exhausted PRN gabapentin and paracetamol without relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Amputation (with acute stump ulceration and phantom pain flare) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Deep Vein Thrombosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to absence of swelling, calf tenderness, or engorged superficial veins in the contralateral limb.

Osteomyelitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the superficial nature of the ulcer, short symptom duration, and absence of systemic toxicity or high-grade fever.

Complex Regional Pain Syndrome - Ruled out/unlikely due to the localized nature of the pain to the neuroma and ulcer, without diffuse autonomic or trophic skin changes beyond the immediate mechanical friction site.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient's acute presentation of worsening localized erythema, skin breakdown, and severe phantom limb pain strongly indicates an impending stump infection and acute neuropathic pain crisis. The objective findings of localized warmth and exquisite neuroma tenderness, combined with the subjective rapid escalation of pain over the past 4 hours, confirm that this assessment could not be delayed until the next in-hours period. Immediate, in-person clinical evaluation was mandatory to exclude deep tissue infection, optimize neuropathic pain control, and prevent further mechanical trauma that would lead to hospitalization.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed oral Cephalexin 500mg QID for 7 days. Increased Gabapentin dose for 3 days to manage neuropathic flare. Advised strict prosthetic rest.
Positive Behavioral Change: When you keep the prosthesis off, your skin will heal faster; therefore, leave the prosthesis off completely for the next 72 hours and reward yourself with an activity you enjoy while resting to reinforce this habit. When you inspect the stump daily with a hand mirror, you catch redness early; doing this every morning builds a consistent self-monitoring routine.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to community nursing for wound dressings.
DME/Safety (Optional): Recommended removal of loose rugs in the hallway.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3 days for wound check and medication titration.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the nearest Emergency Department or call 000 if you develop a fever above 38.5 C, if the redness spreads rapidly up the leg, or if you experience spreading purulent discharge and confusion.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending response to antibiotics and rest; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: Expected natural course is gradual reduction in pain and erythema over 48-72 hours with antibiotic therapy and strict rest. Continued friction will delay healing.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if pain becomes completely unmanageable within 12 hours, or if the redness expands beyond the marked pen line by tomorrow morning.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The antibiotic will treat the superficial skin infection, while resting without the prosthesis prevents further friction. The increased gabapentin directly targets the misfiring nerves causing the phantom pain.
Actionable Steps: Keep the prosthesis off. Take the antibiotics at the same times each day. Inspect the stump daily.

